                },
                {
                  "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
                  "ShowInDocs": false,
                  "Name": "HeightGrowth",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                      "Text": "Mean tree height (m) as an incremental state, replacing the Age-based height equation. Updated daily in the TreeThinning script (Script.Height): dH/dt = Rate x Shape x H x ((MaximumHeight/H)^(1/Shape) - 1) x (FT x FVPD x FN x FW)^ModifierExponent x CoverGreen^CoverExponent / 365 (age-free Chapman-Richards), starting at InitialHeight at planting; thinning or mortality multiplies H by (retained/previous mean DBH)^ThinningExponent (within-stand H-D exponent 0.73), so thinning from below raises mean height. Height is largely independent of stocking above about 300 stems/ha (Waghorn et al. 2007 radiata Nelder trial: H 16.6-18.4 m across 209-2551 sph at age 17; STAG E. grandis Nelder wheel, Rance) and lower at very wide spacing (Maclaren et al. 1995; Mason 2023); the canopy-cover term reproduces the wide-spacing and young-stand penalty, and diameter carries the stocking response (DBH from volume and height). Calibration 2026-11-14: radiata Rate 0.0583, Shape 0.903, MaximumHeight 45, exponents 0.3 (fitted to 321 validation heights, NSE 0.97; exponent set so height at 20 y scales with productivity as in 378 SwYG PSP radiata plots, ln H20 vs ln VMAI20 slope 0.22, model 0.26; PSP Green Triangle radiata median height 27 m at 20 y, 32 m at 30 y); taeda Rate 0.2106, Shape 3.35, MaximumHeight 35, ModifierExponent 1.81, CoverExponent 0 (SETRES, IMPAC loblolly; NSE 0.94); caribaea, hybrids and slash Rate 0.07, Shape 0.52, MaximumHeight 35, exponents 0.65 (FPQ, HQP; NSE 0.98; slash IMPAC plots have no height data). MaximumHeight is weakly constrained (radiata literature 45-65 m). Known gaps: FSA703A (N-starved, LAI ~2) observed volume and basal area imply ~18-20 m at 32 y vs ~30 m simulated; Puruki early heights high.\n\nSlenderness limit: height growth is multiplied by a factor that is 1 while the mean-tree height/DBH ratio (H/D, m/m) is below SlendernessOnset and falls linearly to 0 at SlendernessLimit. Height is close to stocking-neutral over normal stockings, but trees cannot keep gaining height without diameter: in 15,152 SwYG radiata PSP measurements H/Dq never exceeds 155, and the 99th percentile is 129-146 at 800-2500 stems/ha. Without the limit, stress tests at 5000 stems/ha or a quarter of the rainfall gave H/D 200-370 by late rotation, which kept DBH and SDI too small for self-thinning to start.",
                      "Name": "Memo",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.Constant, Models",
                      "FixedValue": 0.3,
                      "Units": "m",
                      "Name": "InitialHeight",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
